      "key": "prose/4",
      "text": "For an ideal eye without refractive error, accommodation can be relaxed when viewing a distant object while its image still falls on the retina.\nThe ciliary muscle is a ring of muscle around the lens, and the suspensory fibers are fine strands attached to the lens.\n\nFor near viewing, this muscle contracts and the fibers pull less on the lens.\nThe lens becomes rounder, shortening the eye's equivalent focal length so that the image falls on the retina again.\n\nThe source calls this lens compression, but the intended focusing change should be understood as a muscle-mediated change in curvature, not compression of the lens as a whole body.\nThis adjustment of focusing ability to the distance of the object is called accommodation.\n\nFor two object points with a fixed separation, moving the object closer increases the angle they subtend at the eye, the visual angle $\\varphi$ (Figure 185).\nA larger visual angle helps separate detail, and the source uses an angle greater than $1$ arcminute as a rough reference for distinguishing two points.\n\nActual resolution also depends on illumination, contrast, and retinal and neural processing, so images falling on different photoreceptors alone do not guarantee that two separate points will be seen.\nMoving the object ever closer therefore does not always make it clearer, because accommodation has a limit.\nThe nearest object point that can still be brought into clear focus under given conditions is the near point of accommodation, and a closer object cannot be made clear simply by accommodating further, even though its visual angle is larger.",
