      "key": "prose/14",
      "text": "A converging lens changes incoming ray directions and explains the optical principle of hyperopia-correcting spectacles.\nFirst specify what the eye's focusing muscles are doing, for example whether they are relaxed or adjusting focus.\n\nIn the distant-viewing model, the spectacle lens and the eye together must focus approximately parallel incoming rays on the retina (Figure 187(b)).\nThis happens only when the lens and the eye's focusing ability match in that chosen state.\n\nIn the source's near-viewing construction, light from an object $25$ cm from the eye (Figure 187(c)) becomes less divergent after passing through the converging lens.\nBackward extensions of the rays reaching the eye appear to come from a virtual image at a distance $d>25$ cm, which the eye treats as a more distant object when focusing.\n\nUnder the conditions of this construction, a suitable converging lens lets the eye see clearly at the conventional distance of distinct vision without adjusting its focus as strongly as it would without the spectacles.\nThe source's conclusion that this distance becomes the same as for a normal eye describes that simplified example, not identical comfort for everyone or advice for choosing spectacle lenses.",
